{{see also|lauch}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|louch}}, from {{inh|de|goh|louh}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*laukaz}}. Compare {{cog|nds|Look}}, {{m|nds|look}}, {{cog|nl|look}}, {{cog|en|leek}}, {{cog|da|løg}}, {{cog|sv|lök}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|[laʊ̯χ]}}
* {{audio|de|De-Lauch.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|m,(e)s}}

# [[leek]]
#: {{syn|de|Porree|Breitlauch|Winterlauch}}

====Usage notes====
* The synonymous term {{m|de|Porree}} is dominant in northern and central Germany, while ''Lauch'' is dominant in southern Germany and Switzerland; in Austria, neither is dominant. Around the turn of the 21st century, ''Lauch'' permeated the whole language area to some degree, barring rural areas. The [https://www.atlas-alltagssprache.de/runde-2/f08/ Atlas der deutschen Alltagssprache] contains further mapping as for the 2010s situation. <!--clarify -- this is not clear English-->

